schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Most crypto losses come from human error, not technical failure
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "Do not ignore human error. Most crypto losses come from user mistakes,
    sending to wrong addresses, falling for phishing, accidentally exposing
    seed phrases. Technical security cannot fix human error without
    training."
  evidence: >-
    No specific study, dataset, or incident report is cited in the article
    body. Article frontmatter lists `sources: []` — no citation attached.
  source_lineage: "src/content/docs/security-best-practices-orgs.md (frontmatter sources field is empty)"
  interpretation: >-
    Used to justify the article's emphasis on team training as "your most
    important investment," positioned alongside (not instead of) technical
    controls like multi-sig and cold storage.
  uncertainty: >-
    Directionally consistent with widely-reported industry patterns (phishing
    and user error are frequently cited as leading loss vectors across
    various third-party crypto-crime reports), but no specific source,
    percentage, or dataset is provided in this article, and its own
    `sources: []` field confirms nothing was attached. Treat as plausible
    industry consensus, not a sourced statistic.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/security-best-practices-orgs.md
    transformation: quoted
    authorship: ai-assisted
